# Tandoor stack secrets (services VM)
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/tandoor.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md, verified empirically (finding #30): Compose
# INTERPOLATES env_file values, so any value containing '$' must be
# single-quoted before encrypting. `openssl rand -hex` cannot emit one;
# Django's own get_random_secret_key() CAN, so quote that if you use it.
#
# ⚠ These are BARE Django names on purpose (`SECRET_KEY`, `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`),
# which is exactly why Tandoor and Wger are separate stacks: both read those
# same names out of their own environment, and `env_file` hands every service
# in a project the whole .env.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🚨 SECRET_KEY unset does NOT crash. recipes/settings.py falls back to the
# literal 'INSECURE_STANDARD_KEY_SET_IN_ENV' — a string in the PUBLIC
# repository — and boot.sh prints a [WARNING] and carries on. Every session
# cookie and password-reset token would then be signed with a published key,
# and because the fallback is CONSTANT nothing looks wrong across restarts.
# Same class as finding #17 (Kavita's placeholder TokenKey).
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECRET_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres. The rest of the database settings live in compose.yaml ON PURPOSE:
# with DB_ENGINE and POSTGRES_DB absent, Tandoor silently falls back to an
# EPHEMERAL SQLite file inside the container and boot.sh does not even wait for
# Postgres. Keeping them out of here means a lost .env produces an
# authentication error rather than a phantom database. See the compose header.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The first superuser, created by tandoor_init.
#
# There is no env-var path and no REST path upstream: `/setup/` is a browser
# form that refuses to run once any user exists, and there is no management
# command for it. tandoor_init drives the ORM through `manage.py shell`, which
# is the same path the form takes — and then calls the app's OWN
# `create_space_for_user`, so the admin's Space exists before anyone logs in
# rather than being materialised by middleware on first request.
#
# ⚠ Do NOT reach for `manage.py seed_basic_data` as a shortcut. Its --help
# reads "Seeds some basic data (space, account, food)" and it is a DEVELOPER
# FIXTURE: it creates a user literally named `test` with password `test` and a
# Space called "Test Space".
# Generate the password: openssl rand -base64 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TANDOOR_ADMIN_USER=idan
TANDOOR_ADMIN_EMAIL=idan@idanreed.com
TANDOOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password
